A patient presents with the following case:

A 62-year-old postmenopausal woman with metastatic HR-positive/HER2-negative breast cancer returns after 4 cycles of inpatient paclitaxel started for visceral crisis, when she initially presented with rapidly progressive liver metastases causing jaundice, dyspnea, and marked transaminitis. She now reports improved appetite and breathing, and her right upper quadrant discomfort has largely resolved. On exam she is hemodynamically stable, no longer in respiratory distress, and has only mild hepatomegaly without ascites. Labs show total bilirubin improved from 4.1 to 1.1 mg/dL, AST 48 U/L, ALT 52 U/L, and alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L, with interval imaging showing decreased hepatic tumor burden. She has no major comorbidities limiting systemic therapy and is being seen after clinical stabilization following chemotherapy.

